<TMU|<tuple|1.1.0|2026.3.0>>

<style|<tuple|generic|table-captions-above|number-europe|preview-ref>>

<\body>
  <\question*>
    Why <em|normal equation> <math|A<rsup|\<top\>>A<wide|x|~>=A<rsup|\<top\>>b> is always solvable?
  </question*>

  <\proof>
    Noticed that

    <\equation*>
      rank<around*|(|A|)>=rank<around*|(|A<rsup|\<top\>>A|)>\<leqslant\>rank<around*|(|<bmatrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsup|\<top\>>A>|<cell|A<rsup|\<top\>>b>>>>>|)>=rank<around*|(|A<rsup|\<top\>><bmatrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A>|<cell|b>>>>>|)>\<leqslant\>rank<around*|(|A<rsup|\<top\>>|)>=rank<around*|(|A|)>,
    </equation*>

    then every inequality signs above is equality, so

    <\equation*>
      rank<around*|(|A<rsup|\<top\>>A|)>=rank<around*|(|<bmatrix|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|A<rsup|\<top\>>A>|<cell|A<rsup|\<top\>>b>>>>>|)>\<Longrightarrow\><with|color|red|A<rsup|\<top\>>b\<in\>\<cal-C\><around*|(|A<rsup|\<top\>>A|)>>.
    </equation*>

    Then it's clear that <math|A<rsup|\<top\>>A<wide|x|~>=A<rsup|\<top\>>b> is solvable.
  </proof>

  \;

  If the columns of <math|A> are linearly independent (<math|A<rsup|\<top\>>A> is invertible), then

  <\equation*>
    <wide|x|~>=<around*|(|A<rsup|\<top\>>A|)><rsup|-1>A<rsup|\<top\>>b.
  </equation*>
</body>

